Do not touch the test driver target when it was not created

MITK_CREATE_MODULE_TESTS() accepts PACKAGE_DEPENDS and passes them to
mitk_create_executable(), which correctly declines to create the test driver
when a package dependency is unmet -- the same way MITK_CREATE_MODULE() declines
to build a module. The macro then unconditionally does

    set_property(TARGET ${EXECUTABLE_TARGET} PROPERTY FOLDER ...)

on a target that does not exist, turning a dependency that should simply be
skipped into a hard configure failure for the whole project:

    CMake Error at CMake/mitkMacroCreateModuleTests.cmake:45 (set_property):
      set_property could not find TARGET MitkMultilabelTestDriver.
      Perhaps it has not yet been created.
    Call Stack (most recent call first):
      Modules/Multilabel/Testing/CMakeLists.txt:1 (MITK_CREATE_MODULE_TESTS)

Modules/Multilabel/Testing declares PACKAGE_DEPENDS DCMQI, so any build without
DCMQI cannot configure with BUILD_TESTING=ON at all -- it does not merely lose
the Multilabel tests. A distribution build against system libraries is exactly
that case.

Guard the remainder of the macro on the target actually existing, so an unmet
test dependency skips that module's tests and leaves the rest of the suite
configurable.

--- a/CMake/mitkMacroCreateModuleTests.cmake
+++ b/CMake/mitkMacroCreateModuleTests.cmake
@@ -42,6 +42,13 @@
                            NO_BATCH_FILE
                            NO_INSTALL
                            ${_no_init})
+    # mitk_create_executable() declines to create the driver when a
+    # PACKAGE_DEPENDS entry is unmet. That is a legitimate skip, not an error,
+    # so do not configure tests against a target that does not exist.
+    if(NOT TARGET ${EXECUTABLE_TARGET})
+      message(STATUS "${TESTDRIVER} not created (unmet test dependency); skipping its tests")
+      return()
+    endif()
     set_property(TARGET ${EXECUTABLE_TARGET} PROPERTY FOLDER "${MITK_ROOT_FOLDER}/Modules/Tests")
 
     #
